Kibana is NOT just dashboards & graphs.

A real SOC workflow is:
Logs → Search/Filter → Visualize → Alert → Notification → Action

Instead of constantly watching dashboards, you configure alerts so Kibana tells you when something abnormal happens.
----
# 1. What Are Alerts in Kibana?
Alerts = Rules you define that watch your data continuously.
Example:
     • “If failed login attempts > 20 in 5 minutes → Trigger alert”
     • “If CPU usage > 90% → Alert”
     • “If too many logs from one IP → Alert”

Kibana does not create alerts automatically — we should define:
    • Condition
    • Threshold
    • Time window
    • Action (email / webhook / etc.)
----
# 2. How Kibana Alerts Work ?
Data (Elasticsearch Index)
        ↓
Alert Rule Checks Condition
        ↓
Condition True?
        ↓
Trigger Action (Notification)
---
# 3. Types of Alert Rules :

  1.Threshold Alert
Most common & easiest.
Trigger when value exceeds limit.
Example:
     Log count > X
     Error events > X
     Response time > X

  2.Index Threshold Alert
Triggers based on index data.
Example:
     When number of documents > N
     When avg(field) > value
Used heavily in log monitoring.

  3. Metric Alerts
Used for system monitoring.
Example:
     CPU usage
     Memory usage
     Disk usage

   4.Custom Query Alerts (Powerful)
Uses KQL / Lucene queries.
Example:
     Detect specific IP activity
     Detect error codes
     Detect suspicious behavior
----
# 4. What Is Threshold? 
Threshold = Trigger limit

Example:
     If error logs > 50 → Alert
     If login failures > 10 → Alert

• We must decide: the metric , number, time duration
---
# 5. How Does Kibana Send Notifications?
Alerts trigger Actions.

• Kibana supports integrations like:
Email, Webhook, Slack, Microsoft Teams, PagerDuty, Index action (write alert to ES), Logs / Server logs

• Actual availability depends on configuration.
Example:
Alert fires → Kibana executes action → Email sent.
----
# 6. Discover 
Discover = Raw log exploration tool.

Used for:
  • Searching logs
  • Filtering logs
  • Investigating incidents
  • Writing KQL queries

Example filters:

source.ip : "192.168.1.10"
event.code : "4625"
log.level : "error"

Discover = SOC analyst’s primary investigation screen
----
# 7. Visualize & Dashboard

Visualize → Create graphs
Dashboard → Combine graphs
  • More logs → More data → More graphs → Better monitoring
----
# 8. KQL (Kibana Query Language)
Used for filtering & alerts.

Basic syntax: field_name : "value"
Example:      source.ip : "10.0.0.5"
Meaning → Show logs only from this IP.
-----
# 9.Security Controls & Access Controls 

Kibana security includes:
   • User authentication
   • Roles
   • Privileges
   • Space restrictions
Example:
   Analyst → Read-only dashboards
   Admin → Full access
   SOC Intern → Discover + Dashboard only
• This prevents misuse & protects data.
-----
# 10. Pipelines 
Pipeline = Log ingestion flow.

Examples:
    Filebeat → Elasticsearch
    Logstash → Elasticsearch
    Beats → Logstash → Elasticsearch

They control:Parsing , Enrichment, Forwarding
---
# 11. AI & Advanced Features 
Kibana + Elastic Stack offers:
    • Anomaly detection
    • ML jobs
    • Pattern recognition
    • Outlier detection
Used for detecting abnormal log behavior.
-----
Kibana is not only for visualization. We can create alert rules that continuously monitor Elasticsearch indices. 
Alerts are triggered based on thresholds or conditions, and actions can be configured such as 
email or webhook notifications. Discover is used for raw log analysis using KQL filters. 
Security controls help restrict user access. Pipelines like Filebeat/Logstash control log ingestion. 
Alerting improves SOC efficiency because analysts do not need to constantly watch dashboards.

